@@@ problem 141 linked-list-cycle
title: Linked List Cycle
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, Linked List, Two Pointers
params: head, pos
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_nodes

=== description
Given `head`, the head of a linked list, determine whether the list has a **cycle**: some node can be reached again by following `next` pointers.

In the input, `pos` is the index of the node the tail's `next` points to, or `-1` for no cycle. `pos` is only used to build the list; it is **not** passed to your function.

Follow-up: can you solve it with O(1) memory?

=== constraints
- `0 <= number of nodes <= 10^4`
- `-10^5 <= Node.val <= 10^5`
- `pos` is `-1` or a valid index

=== hints
- A hash set of visited nodes works, but needs O(n) memory.
- Floyd's tortoise and hare: move one pointer by 1 and another by 2. If there is a cycle they must meet.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(NULL) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        auto vals = c.arg<std::vector<int>>();
        int pos = c.arg<int>();
        ListNode *head = lc::make_list(vals);
        if (pos >= 0 && pos < (int)vals.size()) {
            ListNode *tail = head, *target = head;
            while (tail->next) tail = tail->next;
            for (int i = 0; i < pos; ++i) target = target->next;
            tail->next = target;
        }
        bool r = false;
        Solution s;
        c.time([&] { r = s.hasCycle(head); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        return lc::lines(g.ints(n, -100000, 100000), g.coin(0.5) || n == 0 ? -1 : g.irange(0, n - 1));
    });
}

=== approach
**Floyd's cycle detection.** Move `slow` one step and `fast` two steps at a time. Without a cycle, `fast` reaches the end. With a cycle, both pointers eventually circle inside it, and since `fast` gains one node per step on `slow`, it must land on `slow` within one lap. Only two pointers are used: O(1) memory.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;
        while (fast && fast->next) {
            slow = slow->next;           // 1 step
            fast = fast->next->next;     // 2 steps
            if (slow == fast) return true;   // fast lapped slow: cycle
        }
        return false;                    // fast fell off the end
    }
};

=== examples
[3,2,0,-4]
1
> true
: The tail connects back to the node at index 1.

[1,2]
0
> true

[1]
-1
> false

=== tests
[]
-1
> false

[1]
0
> true

[1,2,3,4,5]
-1
> false

[1,2,3,4,5]
4
> true

@@@ problem 2 add-two-numbers
title: Add Two Numbers
difficulty: Medium
topics: Linked List, Math, Recursion
params: l1, l2
time: O(max(m, n))
space: O(1) extra (output excluded)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=digits_per_number

=== description
Two non-negative integers are stored as linked lists with their digits in **reverse order**: the head holds the ones digit. Add the numbers and return the sum as a linked list in the same format.

Neither number has leading zeros, except the number `0` itself.

=== constraints
- each list has `1..100` nodes
- `0 <= Node.val <= 9`
- no leading zeros except for `0`

=== hints
- This is grade-school addition: add digit by digit and carry.
- Keep going while either list has digits **or** a carry remains.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* addTwoNumbers(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::addTwoNumbers);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto num = [&](int len) {
            auto v = g.ints(std::max(1, len), 0, 9);
            if (v.size() > 1 && v.back() == 0) v.back() = g.irange(1, 9);
            if (g.coin(0.2)) for (auto &d : v) d = 9, v.back() = 9;
            return v;
        };
        return lc::lines(num(n), num(g.coin() ? n : g.irange(1, n)));
    });
}

=== approach
Walk both lists together like column addition. At each step, sum the two current digits (0 if a list has ended) plus the `carry`; append `sum % 10` to the result and keep `sum / 10` as the new carry. Continue while either list has nodes or a carry is left (e.g. `5 + 5 = 10` needs an extra node).

A **dummy head** node avoids special-casing the first result node.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* addTwoNumbers(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {
        ListNode dummy;                 // result starts at dummy.next
        ListNode *tail = &dummy;
        int carry = 0;
        while (l1 || l2 || carry) {
            int sum = carry;
            if (l1) { sum += l1->val; l1 = l1->next; }
            if (l2) { sum += l2->val; l2 = l2->next; }
            tail->next = new ListNode(sum % 10);   // current digit
            tail = tail->next;
            carry = sum / 10;                      // 0 or 1
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }
};

=== examples
[2,4,3]
[5,6,4]
> [7,0,8]
: 342 + 465 = 807.

[0]
[0]
> [0]

[9,9,9,9,9,9,9]
[9,9,9,9]
> [8,9,9,9,0,0,0,1]

=== tests
[5]
[5]
> [0,1]

[1,8]
[0]
> [1,8]

[9]
[1,9,9,9]
> [0,0,0,0,1]

@@@ problem 21 merge-two-sorted-lists
title: Merge Two Sorted Lists
difficulty: Easy
topics: Linked List, Recursion
params: list1, list2
time: O(m + n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=total_nodes

=== description
Merge two sorted linked lists `list1` and `list2` into one **sorted** list by **splicing together** their nodes. Return the head of the merged list.

=== constraints
- each list has `0..50` nodes
- `-100 <= Node.val <= 100`
- both lists are sorted in non-decreasing order

=== hints
- Use a dummy head and a tail pointer; repeatedly attach the smaller front node.
- When one list runs out, attach the rest of the other in one step.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* list1, ListNode* list2) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::mergeTwoLists);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int a = g.irange(0, n);
        return lc::lines(g.sorted_ints(a, -100, 100), g.sorted_ints(n - a, -100, 100));
    });
}

=== approach
Iterative merge with a **dummy node**: keep a `tail` pointer to the last node of the merged list; attach whichever front node is smaller and advance that list. When one list is empty, the other is already sorted, so link it in one step. No new nodes are created: O(1) extra memory. (A recursive version is shorter but uses O(m + n) stack.)

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* list1, ListNode* list2) {
        ListNode dummy;
        ListNode *tail = &dummy;
        while (list1 && list2) {
            if (list1->val <= list2->val) { tail->next = list1; list1 = list1->next; }
            else                          { tail->next = list2; list2 = list2->next; }
            tail = tail->next;
        }
        tail->next = list1 ? list1 : list2;   // append the leftover part
        return dummy.next;
    }
};

=== examples
