1
> [1]

[1,2]
2
> [2,1]

[1,2,3,4,5,6]
3
> [3,2,1,6,5,4]

[1,2,3,4,5]
1
> [1,2,3,4,5]

@@@ problem 19 remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list
title: Remove Nth Node From End of List
difficulty: Medium
topics: Linked List, Two Pointers
params: head, n
time: O(L)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=list_length

=== description
Given the `head` of a linked list, remove the `n`-th node **from the end** of the list and return its head.

Follow-up: can you do it in one pass?

=== constraints
- the list has `sz` nodes, `1 <= sz <= 30`
- `0 <= Node.val <= 100`
- `1 <= n <= sz`

=== hints
- Move a `fast` pointer `n` steps ahead, then move `fast` and `slow` together until `fast` reaches the end.
- A dummy head handles removing the first node.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* removeNthFromEnd(ListNode* head, int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::removeNthFromEnd);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        return lc::lines(g.ints(n, 0, 100), g.irange(1, n));
    });
}

=== approach
**Two pointers with a gap.** Start both at a dummy node before the head. Advance `fast` by `n + 1` steps. Now move both until `fast` is null: `slow` is then exactly one node **before** the target, so `slow->next = slow->next->next` removes it. One pass, O(1) memory. The dummy makes removing the head work without a special case.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* removeNthFromEnd(ListNode* head, int n) {
        ListNode dummy(0, head);
        ListNode *fast = &dummy, *slow = &dummy;
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) fast = fast->next;   // gap of n+1 nodes
        while (fast) {                                    // move together
            fast = fast->next;
            slow = slow->next;
        }
        slow->next = slow->next->next;                    // unlink the target
        return dummy.next;
    }
};

