=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::deleteDuplicates);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.sorted_ints(n, -n / 2 - 2, n / 2 + 2)); });
}

=== approach
Keep `prev`, the last node known to be kept (starting at a dummy). Look at `prev->next`: if it starts a run of equal values (its next has the same value), skip past the entire run and link `prev` to the node after it. Otherwise the node is unique, so advance `prev`. Every node is looked at a constant number of times.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteDuplicates(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode dummy(0, head);
        ListNode *prev = &dummy;                   // last node that is definitely kept
        while (prev->next) {
            ListNode *cur = prev->next;
            if (cur->next && cur->next->val == cur->val) {
                int v = cur->val;
                while (cur && cur->val == v) cur = cur->next;   // skip the whole run
                prev->next = cur;
            } else {
                prev = cur;                        // cur is unique: keep it
            }
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,3,3,4,4,5]
> [1,2,5]

[1,1,1,2,3]
> [2,3]

=== tests
[]
> []

[1,1]
> []

[1,2,2]
> [1]

[1,2,3]
> [1,2,3]

@@@ problem 61 rotate-list
title: Rotate List
difficulty: Medium
topics: Linked List, Two Pointers
params: head, k
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_nodes

=== description
Given the `head` of a linked list, rotate the list to the **right** by `k` places and return the new head.

`k` can be much larger than the length of the list.

=== constraints
- `0 <= n <= 500`
- `-100 <= Node.val <= 100`
- `0 <= k <= 2 * 10^9`

=== hints
- Rotating by the list length changes nothing: use `k % n`.
- Close the list into a ring, then cut it at the right place.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::rotateRight);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        return lc::lines(g.ints(n, -100, 100), g.coin(0.2) ? g.irange(0, 2000000000) : g.irange(0, 3 * n + 1));
    });
}

=== approach
Find the length `n` and the tail in one walk. Reduce `k %= n`; if it's 0, nothing changes. Otherwise link the tail to the head, forming a **ring**, then walk `n - k` steps from the old tail: that node becomes the new tail, and its successor the new head. Cut the ring there.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {
        if (!head || !head->next) return head;
        int n = 1;
        ListNode *tail = head;
        while (tail->next) { tail = tail->next; ++n; }   // length and tail
        k %= n;
        if (k == 0) return head;
        tail->next = head;                                // close the ring
        ListNode *newTail = tail;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - k; ++i) newTail = newTail->next;
        ListNode *newHead = newTail->next;
        newTail->next = nullptr;                          // cut the ring
        return newHead;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,3,4,5]
2
> [4,5,1,2,3]

[0,1,2]
4
> [2,0,1]

=== tests
[]
0
> []

[1]
99
> [1]

[1,2]
2
> [1,2]

[1,2,3]
2000000000
> [2,3,1]

@@@ problem 86 partition-list
title: Partition List
difficulty: Medium
topics: Linked List, Two Pointers
params: head, x
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_nodes

=== description
Given the `head` of a linked list and a value `x`, rearrange it so that all nodes with values **less than** `x` come before the nodes with values **greater than or equal to** `x`.

Keep the original relative order inside each of the two parts.

=== constraints
- `0 <= n <= 200`
- `-100 <= Node.val <= 100`
- `-200 <= x <= 200`

=== hints
- Build two separate lists (with dummy heads) as you walk, then join them.
- Don't forget to terminate the second list: its last node may still point somewhere.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* partition(ListNode* head, int x) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::partition);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.ints(n, -10, 10), g.irange(-12, 12)); });
}

=== approach
Walk the list once and append each node either to a **"less" list** or to a **"greater or equal" list**, both with dummy heads. Appending preserves the relative order. Finally connect the end of the first list to the start of the second, and set the second list's tail `next` to `nullptr` (it may still point to an old successor). Nodes are only relinked, not copied.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* partition(ListNode* head, int x) {
        ListNode lessHead, geHead;
        ListNode *less = &lessHead, *ge = &geHead;
        for (ListNode *p = head; p; p = p->next) {
            if (p->val < x) { less->next = p; less = p; }   // stable: append in order
            else            { ge->next = p;   ge = p; }
        }
        ge->next = nullptr;              // cut off the old successor
        less->next = geHead.next;        // join the two parts
        return lessHead.next;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,4,3,2,5,2]
3
> [1,2,2,4,3,5]

[2,1]
2
> [1,2]

