[]
0
> []

[1]
0
> [1]

[3,1,2]
4
> [3,1,2]

[5,5,1,5]
5
> [1,5,5,5]

@@@ problem 146 lru-cache
title: LRU Cache
difficulty: Medium
topics: Hash Table, Linked List, Design, Doubly-Linked List
params: operations, arguments
time: O(1) per operation
space: O(capacity)
bench: n=64..524288 time=n space=n unit=number_of_operations_(capacity_=_n/4)

=== description
Design a data structure for a **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache**:

- `LRUCache(int capacity)` creates a cache holding at most `capacity` entries.
- `int get(int key)` returns the value for `key`, or `-1` if it is not present.
- `void put(int key, int value)` inserts or updates `key`. If this makes the cache exceed its capacity, evict the **least recently used** key.

Both `get` and `put` count as a "use" of the key and must run in **O(1)** average time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= capacity <= 3000`
- `0 <= key <= 10^4`, `0 <= value <= 10^5`
- at most `2 * 10^5` calls to `get` and `put`

=== hints
- A hash map gives O(1) lookup; a doubly linked list keeps the usage order and allows O(1) removal from the middle.
- In C++, `std::list` plus `unordered_map<int, list<...>::iterator>` does it; `splice` moves a node to the front in O(1).

=== template
class LRUCache {
public:
    LRUCache(int capacity) {

    }

    int get(int key) {

    }

    void put(int key, int value) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.design<LRUCache, int>()
        .method("get", &LRUCache::get)
        .method("put", &LRUCache::put);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        lc::Ops ops;
        int cap = std::max(1, n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 4) : n / 4);
        int keys = std::max(2, n <= 40 ? 6 : n / 2);
        ops.add("LRUCache", cap);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (g.coin(0.5)) ops.add("put", g.irange(0, keys), g.irange(0, 100000));
            else ops.add("get", g.irange(0, keys));
        }
        return ops.lines();
    });
}

=== approach
Combine two structures:

- a **doubly linked list** of `(key, value)` pairs ordered by recency: most recently used at the front;
- a **hash map** from key to its list node (iterator).

`get`: look up the node; if found, move it to the front (`splice`, O(1)) and return its value. `put`: if the key exists, update the value and move it to the front; otherwise insert a new node at the front, and if the size exceeds the capacity, remove the **last** node (the least recently used) from both the list and the map.

=== solution
class LRUCache {
    int cap;
    list<pair<int, int>> order;                                // front = most recent
    unordered_map<int, list<pair<int, int>>::iterator> where;  // key -> node in `order`

public:
    LRUCache(int capacity) : cap(capacity) {}

    int get(int key) {
        auto it = where.find(key);
        if (it == where.end()) return -1;
        order.splice(order.begin(), order, it->second);   // mark as most recent, O(1)
        return it->second->second;
    }

    void put(int key, int value) {
        auto it = where.find(key);
        if (it != where.end()) {                           // update existing entry
            it->second->second = value;
            order.splice(order.begin(), order, it->second);
            return;
        }
        order.emplace_front(key, value);                   // new entry at the front
        where[key] = order.begin();
        if ((int)order.size() > cap) {                     // evict least recently used
            where.erase(order.back().first);
            order.pop_back();
        }
    }
};

=== examples
["LRUCache","put","put","get","put","get","put","get","get","get"]
[[2],[1,1],[2,2],[1],[3,3],[2],[4,4],[1],[3],[4]]
> [null,null,null,1,null,-1,null,-1,3,4]
: put(3,3) evicts key 2 (key 1 was used more recently); put(4,4) then evicts key 1.

=== tests
["LRUCache","put","get"]
[[1],[2,1],[2]]
> [null,null,1]

["LRUCache","put","put","get","get"]
[[1],[2,1],[3,2],[2],[3]]
> [null,null,null,-1,2]

["LRUCache","put","put","put","get","put","get","get"]
[[2],[1,1],[2,2],[1,10],[1],[3,3],[2],[1]]
> [null,null,null,null,10,null,-1,10]

["LRUCache","get","put","get","put","put","get","get"]
[[2],[2],[2,6],[1],[1,5],[1,2],[1],[2]]
> [null,-1,null,-1,null,null,2,6]
